From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 01:59:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08085/11362] The knob meant to fix induction starvation was
 never wired to the live agent

REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-22 found the induce prompt's transition cap "starves the dynamics half to
roughly one example per action type, producing hardcoded-literal-coordinate memorization instead
of general rules". REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-23 answered with `_induce_transitions_k()` and
`CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_TRANSITIONS_K`.

NOTHING ON THE LIVE PATH EVER CALLED IT. `induce_prompt` carried a literal `k: int = 8`; the only
callers of that resolver were `python/carnot/experiment_*.py`. The knob moved the prompt for
experiments and did nothing for the agent -- diagnosis landed, instrument built, fix never
reached the thing being diagnosed. Third dead knob found this session.

WIRED NOW, DEFAULT "SHOW EVERY TRANSITION". Prompted by Mage-VL (arXiv 2607.24904): once you
encode an anchor frame plus DELTAS, subsampling stops being necessary. `_transitions_block`
already did the first half and subsampled anyway, discarding 17 of 25 transitions.

  game   n  changed   k=8 chars   ALL chars   ratio
  ft09  25        6       4,046       4,046   1.00x   <- already showed everything
  lp85  25        2       4,064       4,064   1.00x   <- already showed everything
  sc25  25        7       2,557       2,718   1.06x
  tn36  25       25       3,218       5,023   1.56x
  tu93  25       25       2,532       4,924   1.94x

Worst case ~1,255 tokens. THE BUDGET IT PROTECTED DOES NOT EXIST: "13,824 available tokens"
appears only in docstrings, nothing computes or enforces a prompt budget, and it predates the
n_ctx 16384->81920 fix. The real bound is 81920/4 - 4096 = 16,384. The cap discarded 68% of the
evidence to save ~4% of a budget nothing was near; the prompt uses under 8% of what is available.

`CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_TRANSITIONS_K=8` restores the shipped prompt BYTE-FOR-BYTE, verified against
the renderer from git. My first attempt failed that check: `changed[:k-len(noops)]` yields 8 where
the old code yields 6 on a game with no inert actions, and I had "verified" it by comparing the
new renderer against itself.

Mage-VL's saliency idea is the DEGRADATION path: if deltas ever exceed budget,
`_select_transitions_for_prompt` drops the least NOVEL transitions by (action, change-shape)
signature rather than truncating positionally. It never binds today.

NOT CLAIMED: action coverage was NOT being lost -- `changed[:6]` already covers every distinct
action on all six games. The gain is more examples of the SAME actions, which is the starvation
-22 described and matters for tn36's positional click mechanic.

A HOLDOUT THAT WOULD HAVE BROKEN SILENTLY: experiment_6018 derives held-out as `full \ shown`,
then raises the env as a leak control. With the default showing everything, `held` goes empty and
`if not held: continue` makes that control a NO-OP THAT STILL PASSES. It and its tests now pin
k=8 as the historical cap they measured against -- byte-identical to what they did.

The REQ-5699-23 parity anchor is INVERTED, not deleted. Added a test that fails if the resolver
is re-hardcoded out of the live path.

FRESHNESS: 7 artifacts acknowledged. The four with rebuild_commands were rebuilt and compared
leaf by leaf: ZERO substantive movement -- every differing key was provenance bookkeeping the
rebuild DELETES, 20 keys whose own verdict is `substantive_values_changed: 0`. Note the ruff
reformat changed the source sha and correctly INVALIDATED a first round of acknowledgements --
the mechanism working as designed; format first, then acknowledge.

NOTE FOR THE NEXT WRITER: match the artifact's existing indent. indent=1 over an indent=2 file
turned a 6-leaf append into +52,572 -52,562. Verified by leaf comparison nothing was lost, redone
at +8 -0.

145 tests pass. Separately observed: this session's earlier acknowledgements on these artifacts
are absent from HEAD, dropped by a concurrent session's rebuild.

